        title: "Three Sisters and Great Cities",
        reading: "Long before 1492, the Americas held tens of millions of people in societies as complex as any in Europe or Asia. In the Valley of Mexico, the Mexica built Tenochtitlan, a city of 200,000 people, larger than London or Seville at the time. In the Mississippi valley, Cahokia rose near present-day St. Louis, home to Monks Mound, an earthen pyramid with a base larger than the Great Pyramid of Giza. In the Andes, the Inca governed an empire of some 10 million people, keeping accounts with knotted cords called quipus. In the Northeast, the Haudenosaunee, the Iroquois Confederacy, bound five nations under the Great Law of Peace, a constitution carried in memory for centuries. Maize, beans, and squash, the Three Sisters, were planted together so each helped the others: corn stalks gave beans a climbing pole, beans fixed nitrogen, and squash leaves shaded the soil. Andean farmers cut terraces into mountainsides and bred thousands of potato varieties. In Mexico's wetlands, farmers built chinampas, mud-and-reed garden beds in shallow lakes, yielding several harvests a year. The friar Bartolome de las Casas, who sailed on Columbus's later voyages, described Taino farming: \"They plant yuca in mounds of earth, and it yields roots as thick as a man's leg. Their canoes bring fish from the sea, and none among them knows hunger. The land gives them everything, and they share it freely among all.\" According to Las Casas, this abundance came from planting with the rains and enriching soils with ash and refuse. These food systems fed cities, armies, and trade networks, and their crops, corn, potatoes, tomatoes, cacao, and tobacco, reshaped diets on every continent. The Americas in 1491 were no wilderness awaiting discovery. They were a managed, farmed, and governed world.",
