        title: "1492: Worlds Collide",
        reading: "On October 12, 1492, three small Spanish ships reached an island in the Bahamas, and two worlds that had developed separately for 15,000 years collided. Columbus believed he had reached Asia, called the Taino people Indians, and claimed their land for Spain. In his journal that day he wrote: \"They are well built with handsome bodies and very good faces. They should be good servants and very intelligent, for I have observed that they quickly repeat what is said to them. I gave them red caps and glass beads which they hung around their necks, and many other things of little value, and they were delighted.\" Within a generation, that delight curdled into catastrophe. The Spanish demanded gold and labor, enslaving Taino villagers in mines and on plantations, and within fifty years the Taino population of Hispaniola fell from perhaps 250,000 to a few thousand. But contact was not only conquest; it was also exchange. The Columbian Exchange moved crops, animals, people, and diseases in both directions across the Atlantic. Europe gained corn, potatoes, tomatoes, cacao, and tobacco, crops that would feed population booms for centuries. The Americas gained wheat, sugarcane, horses, cattle, pigs, and sheep, animals that transformed Native hunting, warfare, and trade. The exchange also carried killers: smallpox, measles, and influenza raced ahead of European soldiers into virgin soil populations with no immunity, emptying villages and toppling empires. Disease did as much conquering as any army; some historians estimate that 90 percent of Native peoples in the hardest-hit regions died in the century after contact. The collision, then, was many things at once: invasion and trade, genocide and garden, catastrophe and exchange. Holding all of those truths together, without letting one erase the others, is the work of honest history.",
