        title: "Silver, Souls, and the Encomienda",
        reading: "By 1600, Spain controlled the largest empire the world had ever seen, stretching from California to Chile, and it ran on two engines: silver and souls. The silver mines of Potosi, in present-day Bolivia, and Zacatecas, in Mexico, poured out so much wealth that Spanish coins became the world's first global currency, accepted from Manila to Amsterdam. But the mines devoured workers. Under the encomienda system, Spanish settlers received grants entitling them to the labor and tribute of Native peoples in their district. In theory, encomenderos owed their workers protection and Christian instruction; in practice, the system became forced labor that worked thousands to death in mines and on plantations. When Native populations collapsed from disease and overwork, colonists imported enslaved Africans to replace them, building the plantation complex on Black labor. The second engine was the mission. Franciscan, Dominican, and Jesuit friars built chains of missions from Florida to California, aiming to convert Native peoples to Catholicism. Missions taught European farming and crafts, but they also concentrated Native families where disease spread quickly and where Spanish soldiers could draft their labor. Some friars, most famously Bartolome de las Casas, denounced the cruelty and argued that Native peoples possessed reason and rights, sparking debates at the Spanish court about the justice of conquest. The crown responded with the New Laws of 1542, which tried to limit encomienda abuses, but colonists in the Americas largely ignored them. Spain's motives mixed genuine religious zeal, thirst for glory, and hunger for wealth, and its outcomes mixed cities, churches, and universities with demographic catastrophe. Judging an empire means weighing what it claimed against what it did.",
