        title: "Jamestown: Hunger and Tobacco",
        reading: "In 1607, 104 English men and boys landed on a swampy peninsula in Virginia and named their fort Jamestown, after their king. They came under a charter from the Virginia Company, a joint-stock company that promised investors profit from gold, a passage to Asia, or anything else of value. The settlers found none of those. They were gentlemen unprepared for farming, they drank brackish river water, and they quarreled with the powerful Powhatan Confederacy whose land they occupied. By the winter of 1609, the Starving Time, only 60 of 500 colonists remained alive, and survivors wrote of eating horses, dogs, rats, and, in at least one documented case, each other. Jamestown survived because of two things: the iron rule of leaders like John Smith, who declared that those who would not work would not eat, and tobacco. John Rolfe discovered that a sweeter West Indian strain of tobacco grew well in Virginia soil, and England's appetite for the leaf was bottomless. Tobacco demanded enormous labor, and the colony filled the need with indenture: English men and women signed contracts binding them to four to seven years of labor in exchange for passage across the Atlantic. Roughly half of all English migrants to the Chesapeake came under indenture, and masters worked them brutally; many died before their terms ended. Tobacco made Virginia profitable, but it also made the colony hungry for workers in a way that would soon reshape the meaning of race itself. A charter written for gold had produced, almost by accident, a plantation society built on bound labor.",
