        title: "The Best Poor Man's Country",
        reading: "Between Puritan New England and the plantation South lay the Middle Colonies: New York, New Jersey, Pennsylvania, and Delaware. They were the most diverse corner of British America, and deliberately so. William Penn, a Quaker who had been jailed in England for his faith, founded Pennsylvania in 1681 as a holy experiment in religious freedom. His Frame of Government promised liberty of conscience to all who believed in God, and settlers poured in: English Quakers, German Mennonites and Lutherans, Scots-Irish Presbyterians, Welsh Baptists, and Jews. Philadelphia, the City of Brotherly Love, grew into the colonies' largest city, a bustling port where a dozen languages could be heard on a single street. The region's farms, worked mostly by family labor rather than enslaved workers, earned it the nickname the breadbasket, and one German settler called Pennsylvania the best poor man's country. But pluralism had limits, and even its champions felt them. Benjamin Franklin, the colony's most famous son, worried in 1751 about German immigration: \"Why should Pennsylvania, founded by the English, become a Colony of Aliens? They will shortly be so numerous as to Germanize us instead of our Anglifying them. They will never adopt our Language or Customs, any more than they can acquire our Complexion.\" According to Franklin, tolerance stopped where cultural comfort ended. The same colony that welcomed dissenters also profited from slavery: Philadelphia merchants traded in enslaved people, and enslaved Black laborers worked the docks and farms. Pluralism in the Middle Colonies was real, broad, and profitable, and it was also bounded, by race, by language, and by the prejudices of even its most enlightened men.",
