        title: "The Plantation South",
        reading: "South of the Chesapeake, the Southern colonies built the most profitable and most brutal economy in British America. Virginia and Maryland grew tobacco, but South Carolina and Georgia found a richer crop: rice. Enslaved Africans from the Rice Coast of West Africa brought the knowledge that made Carolina planters rich, how to dike swamps, flood fields, and thresh grain. Planters called it Carolina Gold, and it bought mansions in Charleston and seats in Parliament. Indigo, a blue dye, added a second fortune. Charleston merchants shipped rice and indigo to London, and the profits flowed back as fine houses, silver, and books. The plantation system shaped everything: great estates worked by dozens or hundreds of enslaved laborers, a small planter elite that dominated politics, and a legal code that treated Black people as property. By 1740, enslaved Africans outnumbered white colonists in South Carolina, a Black majority colony ruled by a white minority. That imbalance terrified planters, and their fear wrote the law. South Carolina's slave codes restricted Black movement, banned enslaved people from gathering in groups, and punished literacy. Planters also hired slave patrols to hunt escapees and break up secret meetings. Yet the very Africans the planters feared carried military knowledge: many had been soldiers in African wars before capture, and they knew the terrain of swamps and forests better than their masters. The plantation South was an empire of rice built on African expertise and African suffering, and its wealth depended on keeping skilled, knowledgeable people in chains. That contradiction could not hold forever, and in 1739 it exploded.",
