        title: "The Great Awakening",
        reading: "By the 1730s, colonial society was prosperous, diverse, and, in the eyes of many ministers, spiritually asleep. Churches were full of members who had inherited their faith but never felt it, and ministers read dry, learned sermons to polite congregations. The Great Awakening shattered that calm. Itinerant preachers began traveling the colonies holding outdoor revivals, and their weapon was emotion. George Whitefield, a young English evangelist with a voice that could reach 20,000 people without amplification, toured the colonies seven times, weeping, shouting, and pleading with sinners to be born again. Jonathan Edwards preached Sinners in the Hands of an Angry God, warning that sinners dangled over hell like spiders over a flame. Edwards spoke quietly, reading from a manuscript, yet listeners clutched pews and cried out in terror. Crowds wept, trembled, and cried out; converts described feeling God's grace strike like lightning. The Awakening split churches into New Lights, who embraced the revivals, and Old Lights, who condemned the emotional excess. Ministers on both sides published pamphlets attacking each other, and the pamphlet war trained colonists in public debate. But its effects ran deeper than theology. Revivals taught ordinary colonists to judge religious authority for themselves, to gather in voluntary associations, and to challenge established power, habits that would soon be turned against Parliament. The Awakening also carried a radical seed: if all souls were equal before God, as the preachers insisted, then slavery and hierarchy stood condemned. Enslaved and free Black colonists heard that message clearly, and they would soon preach it themselves.",
