      title: "The Peace of Paris, 1763",
      reading: "When the Treaty of Paris was signed in 1763, Britain had won the greatest victory in its history. France surrendered Canada and every claim east of the Mississippi River, and Britain suddenly governed a vast North American empire. But the war had nearly doubled Britain's national debt, and Parliament expected the colonies to help pay it. Loose colonial management was over; London would now run its empire with a tighter hand. The first taste of the new policy arrived within months. In October 1763, King George III issued the Royal Proclamation, drawing a boundary along the Appalachians and forbidding colonial settlement west of it. Lands the colonies had fought to conquer were now reserved for Native nations. Settlers who had bled for the frontier read the proclamation as betrayal; Native leaders doubted Britain could keep its promise. They had reason to doubt. That May, before the proclamation was even issued, the Ottawa leader Pontiac united warriors from the Delaware, Shawnee, and other nations and struck British forts across the Great Lakes and Ohio country. At a council before the uprising, he urged resistance to the English. According to a French chronicler who recorded the speech, Pontiac told his brothers that it was important to drive the English from their lands, because the English sought only to destroy the Native peoples while the French had been better neighbors. Enslaved Black laborers worked inside British forts, free Black traders and soldiers moved through the Ohio country, and Black families lived in the frontier towns. When Pontiac's warriors besieged Fort Detroit and Fort Pitt, Black defenders, laborers, and captives appear in the accounts of the fighting. The peace that followed would satisfy none of these communities.",
