    {type:"shortanswer", instructions:"Write 4-6 sentences, then compare your answer with the model.", items:[
      {id:"f-s1", prompt:"Argue: was the violence in Bleeding Kansas a justified response to proslavery fraud, or a tragic failure of democracy? Write 4-6 sentences, then compare your answer with the model.", answer:"The violence was a tragic failure of democracy, because popular sovereignty was supposed to settle Kansas by vote, not by gun. Proslavery fraud corrupted the process first, stuffing ballot boxes with Missouri border ruffians, which made free-state settlers feel the rules had been rigged. Retaliation like Pottawatomie then gave each side a reason to keep killing, and the cycle proved nothing except that force had replaced law. Yet calling it only a failure ignores that the fraud left honest voters with no fair remedy. The deeper lesson is that democracy cannot survive when one side abandons its rules; Kansas bled because the process was broken first."}
    ]}
