          {id:"f-s1", prompt:"Argue: did the color line inside the labor movement help or hurt American workers as a whole? Use evidence from this week's lessons.", answer:"The color line hurt American workers as a whole because it divided the very force that was supposed to unite them. Excluded from craft unions, Black workers were hired by employers as strikebreakers, which broke strikes and held down wages for white and Black laborers alike. Black organizers built their own unions, like the Colored National Labor Union, but separate unions could not wield the power of one united movement. Meanwhile, strikers at Homestead and Pullman learned that employers and even federal troops stood ready to crush them, so labor needed every ally it could get. A movement that had welcomed all workers from the start would have been stronger, and its victories would have lasted longer."}
