          {id:"f-s1", prompt:"Argue: why did the biracial Populist coalition fail, and who benefited from its failure? Use evidence from this week's lessons.", answer:"The coalition failed because racism was used as a weapon to break an alliance that economics had built. Black and white farmers suffered the same falling prices and crushing debts, and organizations like the Colored Farmers' Alliance proved they could organize together. But wealthy Democrats campaigned on white supremacy, convincing poor white farmers that racial loyalty mattered more than shared economic interests. After the 1896 defeat, new state constitutions disfranchised Black voters, locking the failure in place. The beneficiaries were the bankers, planters, and railroad men whose power the Populists had threatened, and they kept it for another sixty years."}
