      {
        title:"Newton's Second and Third Laws",
        reading:"If the first law describes motion when forces balance, the second law describes what happens when they do not. Newton's second law says that an unbalanced force produces acceleration in the direction of the net force, and the relationship is exact: force equals mass times acceleration, or F = ma. Push a 2-kilogram book with a net force of 6 newtons and it accelerates at 3 meters per second squared. Double the force and you double the acceleration; double the mass and the same force produces only half the acceleration. This is why a loaded truck needs a much bigger engine force to speed up as quickly as an empty one. Newton's third law adds the final piece: for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction. When you push on a wall, the wall pushes back on you with exactly the same strength. A rocket rises because its engines blast exhaust gases downward, and the gases shove the rocket upward with equal force. The two forces always act on different objects, which is why they never cancel each other out, even though they are equal in size. To solve real problems, physicists combine these ideas with free-body diagrams. Sketch the object as a dot, draw an arrow for each force, then add the arrows to find the net force. Once you know the net force, F = ma tells you the acceleration, and the third law reminds you where each force's partner is acting. From the flight of a baseball to the orbit of the Moon, these three laws describe nearly every motion you will ever see.",
        groups:[
          {type:"reveal", instructions:"Tap each card to reveal the meaning of a key physics term.", items:[
            {id:"s2-v1", label:"acceleration", content:"how quickly velocity changes; equals net force divided by mass"},
            {id:"s2-v2", label:"action-reaction pair", content:"two equal and opposite forces that act on different objects"},
            {id:"s2-v3", label:"free-body diagram", content:"a sketch showing an object as a dot with arrows for every force on it"}
          ]},
          {type:"shortanswer", instructions:"Write 4-6 sentences, then compare your answer with the model.", items:[
            {id:"s2-s1", prompt:"Argue: Newton's third law means a horse pulling a cart is also being pulled backward by the cart, so motion should be impossible. Explain the flaw in this reasoning.", answer:"The reasoning fails because the action and reaction forces act on different objects. The horse pulls the cart forward, and the cart pulls the horse backward, but the backward pull acts on the horse while the forward pull acts on the cart. Each object's motion depends only on the forces acting on it. The horse moves forward because its hooves push backward on the ground and the ground pushes the horse forward with a larger force than the cart's backward pull. Equal and opposite forces never cancel when they act on different objects."}
          ]},
          {type:"matching", instructions:"Match each situation to the law it best illustrates.", options:[{key:"a", text:"Newton's first law"}, {key:"b", text:"Newton's second law"}, {key:"c", text:"Newton's third law"}], items:[
            {id:"s2-m1", term:"A rocket's engines blast exhaust downward and the rocket rises", answer:"c"},
            {id:"s2-m2", term:"Doubling the force on a cart doubles its acceleration", answer:"b"},
            {id:"s2-m3", term:"A hockey puck keeps sliding at steady speed on smooth ice", answer:"a"}
          ]}
        ,
          {type: "comprehension", instructions: "Answer each question using evidence from this section's reading.", items: [
            {id: "s40-1-c1", question: "Why does doubling the mass halve the acceleration for the same force, and what does this reveal about mass?", answer: "From F = ma, acceleration equals force divided by mass, so a 2-kilogram book pushed with 6 newtons accelerates at 3 meters per second squared, and doubling the mass halves the acceleration. This is why a loaded truck needs a much bigger engine force to speed up like an empty one: mass measures resistance to changes in motion, which is inertia."},
            {id: "s40-1-c2", question: "Why do the equal-and-opposite forces of Newton's third law never cancel each other out?", answer: "The two forces always act on different objects, so they cannot be summed on a single object. When you push on a wall, the wall pushes back on you; a rocket rises because its engines blast exhaust downward and the gases shove the rocket upward with equal force."},
            {id: "s40-1-c3", question: "How would you combine all three laws with a free-body diagram to analyze the flight of a baseball?", answer: "Sketch the ball as a dot and draw an arrow for each force, such as gravity and air drag. Add the arrows tip to tail to find the net force, use F = ma to find the acceleration, and use the third law to identify where each force's partner acts, for example the ball pushing on the air as the air pushes on the ball."}
          ]},
          {type: "argument", instructions: "Write two well-organized paragraphs defending your thesis. Support your claims with evidence and address the counterargument.", items: [
            {id: "s40-1-a1", prompt: "Defend the following thesis in two paragraphs: Newton's third law is the most powerful of the three laws for modern technology, because every rocket launch depends on action-reaction pairs.", guidance: "Thesis to defend: action-reaction pairs, not F = ma, are the foundation of spaceflight and propulsion. Use evidence from the reading (the rocket exhaust example, forces acting on different objects) plus your research on how rockets or jet engines use the third law. Address this counterargument: the second law is more fundamental because it predicts all acceleration; explain why prediction alone cannot explain propulsion without the third law."}
          ]},
          {type: "vocabwrite", instructions: "Use each word in an original sentence that shows its meaning in the context of this week's topic.", items: [
            {id: "s40-1-vw1", prompt: "Use each word in an original sentence that shows its meaning in the context of this week's topic.", words: ["friction", "inertia"]}
          ]}
        ]
      }
