          {id:"f-s1", prompt:"Argue: the Harlem Renaissance proves that culture is a form of power, not just decoration. Defend or challenge this claim.", answer:"Culture is power because the Harlem Renaissance changed what Black Americans believed about themselves and what the nation believed about them. Hughes and Locke rejected the demand for white approval and built an art that declared Black life beautiful on its own terms. Jazz carried that declaration from Harlem dance halls to the whole country, opening doors that marches and petitions had not. The movement gave the civil rights struggles of later decades a language of pride and a proof of Black achievement. Decoration pleases the eye; the Renaissance armed a people with identity. That is power, exercised through poems and horns instead of laws."}
