        {id:"f-s1", prompt:"Argue: power's new face. Did the election of Black mayors in the 1970s change the daily lives of Black residents? Take a clear position and defend it with evidence from this week's lessons.", answer:"The election of Black mayors changed daily life in real but limited ways. Mayors like Maynard Jackson steered city contracts to Black-owned businesses, and others diversified police and fire departments, which meant new jobs and fairer treatment for many families. Those changes touched paychecks and neighborhoods, not just headlines. Yet the mayors could not stop factories from closing or bring back the tax base that white flight carried to the suburbs. Political power opened doors that had been locked, but the economic forces reshaping cities were bigger than any one office, so the transformation was partial and the struggle continued."}
