      title:"America After 2000",
      reading:"On the morning of September 11, 2001, terrorists hijacked four airplanes and struck the World Trade Center and the Pentagon, killing nearly 3,000 people. The attacks reshaped American life: the United States invaded Afghanistan to topple the Taliban, created the Department of Homeland Security, and began screening passengers in ways travelers still experience today. In 2003, President George W. Bush ordered an invasion of Iraq, arguing that dictator Saddam Hussein held weapons of mass destruction; no such weapons were found, and the war dragged on for years. At home, debates over security, torture, and civil liberties divided the country, while Hurricane Katrina in 2005 exposed how disaster struck Black residents of New Orleans hardest. Then in 2008 the housing market collapsed, banks failed, and millions lost jobs and homes in the worst financial crisis since the Great Depression. Against that backdrop, Barack Obama, a young senator from Illinois, won the presidency, becoming the first Black president in American history. On election night in Chicago's Grant Park, he told a vast crowd: 'If there is anyone out there who still doubts that America is a place where all things are possible; who still wonders if the dream of our founders is alive in our time; who still questions the power of our democracy, tonight is your answer. It is the answer told by lines that stretched around schools and churches in numbers this nation has never seen; by people who waited three hours and four hours, many for the very first time in their lives, because they believed that this time must be different; that their voice could be that difference.' President-elect Barack Obama, Grant Park, Chicago, November 4, 2008. For millions, his victory felt like proof that the struggle of earlier generations had opened a new chapter.",
